  "metadata": {
    "title": "General Motors follows Ford's lead into the battery business",
    "byline": null,
    "published_at": null,
    "site_name": "finance.yahoo.com",
    "language": null
  },
  "content": {
    "text": "Yahoo Finance Senior Autos Reporter Pras Subramanian joins Julie Hyman on Market Catalysts to discuss General Motors' (GM) partnership with Peak Energy, placing a big bet on sodium-ion batteries. This comes one month after Ford (F) launched Ford Energy to develop energy storage batteries.\nSo GM uh uh yesterday after the bell talked, uh basically announced they're doing it among a number of initiatives. One of the big ones is the energy storage sort of standalone business uh with a company called Peak, which is a startup. They're going to work on a type of sodium ion uh type of chemistry which allows for batteries that don't require much cooling, uh which is usually the case with high power batteries in a car. But when we talk about just stationary storage, you can have these units uh kind of built up near utilities, data centers, uh things like that where you need uh additional secondary storage that doesn't necessarily need a lot of complex equipment. Uh doesn't need like I said, uh complex uh cooling equipment either. So that's a good uh for those kinds of purposes. So, you know, GM, like I mentioned, Ford has its own Ford energy sub unit now, which actually Ford is putting a lot of money into. Uh and they're using a facility that Ford had had once earmarked for EV batteries. They're saying, hey, we have the technology, we have uh the space, we have the equipment, we can just pivot this. It's going to cost some money, but they think that it's going to be an actual big business for them uh down the line. as we see the EV transition uh slow a little bit in this country. For GM and Ford, this is a way to redeploy some of that capital, maybe use some of the same technology and equipment to make these batteries for a new business. Uh and then eventually when the EVs do catch up, you can maybe start using those facilities to make some more of the higher displacement energy displacement EV batteries that you might need for those cars.\nAre they using so that you mentioned the ion batteries that um GM is using, is Ford using that that same kind of a tech or is there a little bit of a competitive difference between the two?\nI'm I think that's the this is the competitive difference. I think uh Ford is focusing more on the LFP type batteries, which are which are good batteries, but um probably more expensive to make and probably require a little bit more cooling. Uh I believe Tesla is using the LFP model as well. Uh so this is a new thing that that GM is doing that they see as an opportunity to to distinguish themselves and maybe make a cheaper battery. But yeah, I mean, look, Tesla right now is the is the sort of the main player in the Megapack 3 uh ESS Energy store storage system space. I think that's where GM and Ford sort of maybe want to want to go. Maybe they looked at Tesla and saw that as a way to go because you're already building the batteries for cars.",
    "text_blocks": [
      "Yahoo Finance Senior Autos Reporter Pras Subramanian joins Julie Hyman on Market Catalysts to discuss General Motors' (GM) partnership with Peak Energy, placing a big bet on sodium-ion batteries. This comes one month after Ford (F) launched Ford Energy to develop energy storage batteries.",
      "So GM uh uh yesterday after the bell talked, uh basically announced they're doing it among a number of initiatives. One of the big ones is the energy storage sort of standalone business uh with a company called Peak, which is a startup. They're going to work on a type of sodium ion uh type of chemistry which allows for batteries that don't require much cooling, uh which is usually the case with high power batteries in a car. But when we talk about just stationary storage, you can have these units uh kind of built up near utilities, data centers, uh things like that where you need uh additional secondary storage that doesn't necessarily need a lot of complex equipment. Uh doesn't need like I said, uh complex uh cooling equipment either. So that's a good uh for those kinds of purposes. So, you know, GM, like I mentioned, Ford has its own Ford energy sub unit now, which actually Ford is putting a lot of money into. Uh and they're using a facility that Ford had had once earmarked for EV batteries. They're saying, hey, we have the technology, we have uh the space, we have the equipment, we can just pivot this. It's going to cost some money, but they think that it's going to be an actual big business for them uh down the line. as we see the EV transition uh slow a little bit in this country. For GM and Ford, this is a way to redeploy some of that capital, maybe use some of the same technology and equipment to make these batteries for a new business. Uh and then eventually when the EVs do catch up, you can maybe start using those facilities to make some more of the higher displacement energy displacement EV batteries that you might need for those cars.",
      "Are they using so that you mentioned the ion batteries that um GM is using, is Ford using that that same kind of a tech or is there a little bit of a competitive difference between the two?",
      "I'm I think that's the this is the competitive difference. I think uh Ford is focusing more on the LFP type batteries, which are which are good batteries, but um probably more expensive to make and probably require a little bit more cooling. Uh I believe Tesla is using the LFP model as well. Uh so this is a new thing that that GM is doing that they see as an opportunity to to distinguish themselves and maybe make a cheaper battery. But yeah, I mean, look, Tesla right now is the is the sort of the main player in the Megapack 3 uh ESS Energy store storage system space. I think that's where GM and Ford sort of maybe want to want to go. Maybe they looked at Tesla and saw that as a way to go because you're already building the batteries for cars."
    ],
    "html": null
  },
